<main class="shell container-xxl px-3 px-lg-4">
    <section id="applications" class="page-intro">
        <div class="section-heading row g-3 align-items-end">
            <div class="col-lg-7">
                <div class="eyebrow">Operational Value</div>
                <h1>See where Landsat earns its place in real decision-making</h1>
            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-5">
                <p class="body-copy">
                    Landsat matters because it is calibrated, stable, open, and long-running. That makes it useful for water
                    accounting, biomass and disturbance monitoring, city heat, agriculture, hazards, and data fusion.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="row g-4">
            <div class="col-xl-7">
                <div class="panel h-100">
                    <div class="impact-filters" id="impact-filters"></div>
                    <div class="impact-hero" id="impact-hero" aria-live="polite">
                        <p class="body-copy">Loading application data...</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-xl-5">
                <div class="panel h-100">
                    <div class="eyebrow">Complementary Missions</div>
                    <h3 class="mb-2">Landsat is strongest when you understand what it does not try to be</h3>
                    <p class="body-copy mb-3">
                        Landsat is often the reference record. Other missions add higher revisit, water elevation, forest
                        structure, or thermal detail that Landsat alone cannot provide.
                    </p>
                    <div class="complement-grid" id="complement-grid" aria-live="polite">
                        <p class="body-copy">Loading complementary missions...</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="case-study-grid mt-4">
            <article class="case-card">
                <img src="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/vis/a000000/a003600/a003632/et.0700_0.jpg" alt="Landsat evapotranspiration map over irrigated fields in Idaho">
                <div class="eyebrow">Worked Example</div>
                <h3>Irrigation and evapotranspiration</h3>
                <p>Question: which fields are actively consuming water? Use Surface Temperature with optical context. Cooler irrigated fields can indicate evapotranspiration because water changing phase removes energy from the land surface.</p>
                <a class="resource-link" href="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/3632/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA SVS evapotranspiration media</a>
            </article>
            <article class="case-card">
                <img src="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/vis/a010000/a014700/a014712/Firewatch_Thumb.png" alt="NASA Guardian Landsat Firewatch video thumbnail">
                <div class="eyebrow">Worked Example</div>
                <h3>Wildfire severity and recovery</h3>
                <p>Question: where did vegetation lose canopy moisture and structure? Use NIR and SWIR reasoning, then compare pre-fire, post-fire, and recovery dates with clouds masked out.</p>
                <a class="resource-link" href="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/14712/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA SVS firewatch video</a>
            </article>
            <article class="case-card">
                <img src="/img/landsat.jpg" alt="Landsat view of a changing Earth surface">
                <div class="eyebrow">Worked Example</div>
                <h3>Reservoir decline and drought</h3>
                <p>Question: how has open-water extent changed? Use NDWI-style reasoning, shoreline comparison, and seasonal caution so drought, management, and acquisition timing are not confused.</p>
                <a class="resource-link" href="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/15039/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Landsat and HLS time-series gallery</a>
            </article>
            <article class="case-card">
                <img src="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/vis/a000000/a003600/a003632/temperature.0700.jpg" alt="Thermal Landsat image showing temperature differences across irrigated fields">
                <div class="eyebrow">Worked Example</div>
                <h3>Urban heat and impervious surfaces</h3>
                <p>Question: which neighborhoods retain more surface heat? Use Surface Temperature with vegetation and built-up context, then discuss planning decisions such as shade, tree canopy, cool roofs, and pavement.</p>
                <a class="resource-link" href="https://www.usgs.gov/landsat-missions/landsat-collection-2-surface-temperature" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">USGS Surface Temperature</a>
            </article>
            <article class="case-card">
                <img src="https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/esd/eo/images/imagerecords/1000/1396/landsat_aral_triptych_lrg.jpg?w=1200&h=540&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint" alt="Landsat triptych showing the Aral Sea shrinking between 1973 and 2000" style="object-position: left top;">
                <div class="eyebrow">Worked Example</div>
                <h3>The Aral Sea's disappearance</h3>
                <p>Question: how much open water has actually been lost, and over what timescale? Once the world's fourth-largest lake, the Aral Sea lost more than 60% of its area after upstream rivers were diverted for irrigation. Compare Landsat scenes decades apart using NDWI-style reasoning to see the shoreline retreat directly.</p>
                <a class="resource-link" href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/the-shrinking-aral-sea-1396" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Earth Observatory: The Shrinking Aral Sea</a>
            </article>
            <article class="case-card">
                <img src="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/vis/a000000/a002100/a002106/a002106.00170_print.png" alt="Landsat images showing the fishbone deforestation pattern in Rondonia, Brazil">
                <div class="eyebrow">Worked Example</div>
                <h3>Rondônia's "fishbone" deforestation</h3>
                <p>Question: how does a road network turn into large-scale forest loss? A single road cut through Rondônia, Brazil, then side roads branched off at right angles, and clearing spread outward from each one, a pattern visible in Landsat scenes from 1975 through 2001. NBR-style reasoning across dates shows how fast the fishbone pattern can consume a landscape.</p>
                <a class="resource-link" href="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/2106/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA SVS: Deforestation of Rondônia, Brazil</a>
            </article>
            <article class="case-card">
                <img src="https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/esd/eo/images/imagerecords/77000/77938/columbia_tm5_2011120_lrg.jpg" alt="Landsat 5 false-color image of the retreating Columbia Glacier, Alaska">
                <div class="eyebrow">Worked Example</div>
                <h3>Columbia Glacier's rapid retreat</h3>
                <p>Question: what does one of the fastest-retreating glaciers on Earth look like from orbit? Landsat 5 Thematic Mapper images from 1986 and 2011 show the glacier's terminus pulling back after it began a rapid, still-ongoing retreat in 1980. Compare the ice extent and proglacial water directly between dates.</p>
                <a class="resource-link" href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/retreat-of-alaskas-columbia-glacier-77938" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Earth Observatory: Columbia Glacier Retreat</a>
            </article>
            <article class="case-card">
                <img src="https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/esd/eo/images/imagerecords/85000/85880/mtsthelens_oli_2015110_lrg.jpg?w=1200&h=675&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint" alt="Landsat 8 OLI perspective view of Mount St. Helens and its recovering blast zone">
                <div class="eyebrow">Worked Example</div>
                <h3>Mount St. Helens: disturbance and recovery</h3>
                <p>Question: how long does a landscape take to recover from a catastrophic disturbance? Landsat has observed the 1980 eruption's blast zone every year since, and vegetation has crept back across most of it over decades, except the still-bare Pumice Plain. It is a natural counterpart to the fire-recovery reasoning used elsewhere on this page.</p>
                <a class="resource-link" href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/mount-st-helens-at-35-85880" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Earth Observatory: Mount St. Helens at 35</a>
            </article>
        </div>
        <div class="practice-panel mt-4">
            <div>
                <div class="eyebrow">Practice Prompt</div>
                <h3>Choose evidence before choosing a map</h3>
                <p class="body-copy">For each application, ask students to name the land process, the product, the useful bands or index, and one limitation before they interpret the image.</p>
            </div>
            <a class="button button-primary" href="/studio/band-lab">Practice band choices</a>
        </div>
